\section{Options for the command line}

A command that operates on users must let its caller say \emph{which} users.
There are two fundamentally different ways of saying that, and telling them
apart is what this entire section turns on.

\emph{Selection} options answer the question \enquote{which users?}.
We provide two, and they are alternatives:
\begin{description}
\item[The user option] [[-u]] names users directly, by matching a regex
against their identifiers.
\item[The group option] [[-G]] names users indirectly, as the members of the
groups whose names match a regex.
\end{description}
A command works on either a set of users named directly or on the members of a
set of groups; asking for both at once is contradictory, so we make the two
mutually exclusive.

\emph{Filter} options answer a different question: \enquote{given that
selection, which candidates count?}.
Each selection comes with one:
\begin{description}
\item[The role filter] [[-r]] restricts which enrolments we consider when we
look users up; it changes \emph{how} [[-u]] resolves, not \emph{whether} we
select by user.
\item[The category filter] [[-C]] restricts which group categories we search;
it narrows what [[-G]] can match instead of replacing it.
\end{description}
A filter is therefore always used \emph{together with} the selection it
refines---never instead of it.

The distinction is not merely descriptive, it decides where each option must be
registered.
We express \enquote{these are alternatives} with an argparse mutually exclusive
group, and every option placed in such a group becomes unusable together with
every other option in it.
Put a filter in there and it can no longer be combined with the very selection
it exists to refine.
That is exactly the mistake this module used to make; we return to it in
\cref{sec:user-or-group-option}, where the two kinds finally meet.

Because a selection and its filter can end up on \emph{different} parsers, the
helpers below take the parser for the selection as [[parser]] and, optionally,
a separate [[filter_parser]] for the accompanying filter.
Commands that use a single plain parser simply omit the second one.

\subsection{The user option}

We provide the two helper functions for other modules to filter out users from 
a set of courses.
This option requires the course option from [[canvaslms.cli.courses]].

The first function, [[add_user_option_wo_depends]], simply adds the user 
option.
The more useful function, [[add_user_option]], will also try to add the other 
required options, like the course(s) where to find users.
<<functions>>=
def add_user_option_wo_depends(parser, required=False, suppress_help=False,
                               filter_parser=None):
  """
  Adds the -u option to argparse parser, without adding
  other required options.

  Args:
    parser: The parser (or mutually exclusive group) that gets the -u
      selection option
    required: Whether the user option should be required (default: False)
    suppress_help: If True, hide this option from help output (default: False)
    filter_parser: The parser that gets the accompanying -r filter option,
      defaults to parser

  <<user roles option doc>>
  """
  <<default [[filter_parser]] to [[parser]]>>

  help="Filter users on Canvas ID, name, login ID, integration ID, or " \
       "SIS ID by user_regex. " \
       "Integration ID and SIS ID match exactly, not by regex. " \
       "Note that for login ID, you should start with ^ and $ to avoid " \
       "matching on unintended substrings; c.f. son@institution.tld and " \
       "person@institution.tld, where the first will match both without " \
       "leading ^. The regex allows matching using ^son@, thus skipping " \
       "any domain in this case."
  options = {"required": required}
  if not required:
    options["default"] = DEFAULT_USER_REGEX
    help += f", default: '{DEFAULT_USER_REGEX}'"
  else:
    help += ", required: use '.*' to match all students"

  parser.add_argument("-u", "--user", metavar="user_regex",
    help=argparse.SUPPRESS if suppress_help else help, **options)

  <<add user roles option>>

def add_user_option(parser, required=False):
  """Adds the -u option to argparse parser"""
  try:
    courses.add_course_option(parser)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  add_user_option_wo_depends(parser, required)
@

The default regex matches every user, so omitting [[-u]] asks for all of them.
We give that regex a name because it is the only thing that distinguishes
\enquote{the caller left the user selection alone} from \enquote{the caller
asked for these users}, a distinction
[[process_user_or_group_option]] depends on in \cref{sec:user-or-group-option}.
<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_USER_REGEX = ".*"
@

Splitting the selection from its filter is optional: a caller that passes only
one parser gets both options on it, which is what every command built on a
single plain parser wants.
Both option helpers need this fallback, and they name their parameters
identically, so we write it once and share it.
<<default [[filter_parser]] to [[parser]]>>=
if filter_parser is None:
  filter_parser = parser
@

When processing this option, we need to filter by course first, so we use the
processing from the [[courses]] module to get the list of courses matching the 
courses options.
Then we simply filter all users.

\subsubsection{Converting role string to list}

The [[--role]] option (added by [[add_user_option_wo_depends]]) accepts a
single role string like [["student"]] or [["ta"]] via argparse.
However, [[filter_users]] and [[list_users]] expect a list of role names, 
since they need to iterate over roles when checking if any role matches a 
user's enrollments.

If we mistakenly pass a string directly to [[filter_users]], Python will 
iterate over individual characters (for example, [["student"]] becomes 
[['s']], [['t']], [['u']], [['d']], [['e']], [['n']], [['t']]) when checking 
roles.
This causes all enrollment types to match, since characters like [['t']], 
[['a']], [['e']], [['n']] appear in [["StudentEnrollment"]], 
[["TaEnrollment"]], [["TeacherEnrollment"]], and so on.

Therefore, we must convert [[args.role]] to a single-element list before 
passing it to [[filter_users]].
<<functions>>=
def process_user_option(canvas, args):
  """Processes the user option from command line, returns a list of users"""
  # args.role is a single string (e.g., "student"), but filter_users expects
  # a list of role names. Convert it to a single-element list.
  roles_list = [args.role] if args.role else []
  user_list = list(filter_users(
    courses.process_course_option(canvas, args),
    args.user,
    roles=roles_list))
  if not user_list:
    raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError("No users found matching the criteria")
  return user_list
@

We note that we need the roles.
Sometimes we want to be able to filter based on role.
However, most of the time, we just want the students, so we'll default to that.

This is the filter half of the user option, so it goes on [[filter_parser]]
rather than on [[parser]].
Whenever the two differ, [[-u]] is competing with an alternative selection and
must be registered where that competition is enforced, whereas [[-r]] competes
with nothing and must stay outside it---see \cref{sec:user-or-group-option}.
<<user roles option doc>>=
The `role` option allows specifying which roles to include, for instance
students or TAs.
<<add user roles option>>=
filter_parser.add_argument("-r", "--role",
  choices={"teacher", "student", "student_view",
           "ta", "observer", "designer"},
  default="student",
  help=argparse.SUPPRESS if suppress_help else
    "Includes only users in this role, defaults to student.")
@

\subsection{The group option}

The group option is a bit more complex, because the group selection [[-G]]
comes with a filter of its own: the group category [[-C]].
The two \emph{compose}, they are not alternatives.
Filtering on the category first is useful because students can rename a group
so that its name no longer matches the category by prefix; narrowing to the
category recovers those groups, and the group regex then picks among what is
left.
Since [[filter_groups]] turns an omitted group regex into [[".*"]], giving only
[[-C]] means \enquote{every group in these categories}.

The category is thus the group counterpart of the role filter, and it is routed
the same way: the selection goes on [[parser]], the filter on
[[filter_parser]].
<<functions>>=
def add_group_category_option_wo_depends(parser, suppress_help=False):
  """Adds the group category option, without adding required other options

  Args:
    parser: The argparse parser to add options to
    suppress_help: If True, hide this option from help output (default: False)
  """
  parser.add_argument("-C", "--category", metavar="category_regex",
    required=False,
    help=argparse.SUPPRESS if suppress_help else
      "Filters groups only from the group categories matching "
      "category_regex")

def add_group_category_option(parser):
  """Adds the group category option, adds required options"""
  try:
    courses.add_course_option(parser)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  add_group_category_option_wo_depends(parser)

def add_group_option_wo_depends(parser, suppress_help=False,
                                filter_parser=None):
  """
  Adds group filtering option to argparse parser,
  without adding required options

  Args:
    parser: The parser (or mutually exclusive group) that gets the -G
      selection option
    suppress_help: If True, hide this option from help output (default: False)
    filter_parser: The parser that gets the accompanying -C filter option,
      defaults to parser
  """
  <<default [[filter_parser]] to [[parser]]>>

  try:
    add_group_category_option_wo_depends(filter_parser,
      suppress_help=suppress_help)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  parser.add_argument("-G", "--group", metavar="group_regex",
    required=False,
    help=argparse.SUPPRESS if suppress_help else
      "Filters user groups whose name match group_regex")

def add_group_option(parser):
  """Adds group filtering options to argparse parser,
  adds required other options"""
  try:
    courses.add_course_option(parser)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  add_group_option_wo_depends(parser)
@

We must also provide a function that can process these options.
We ask Canvas to embed each group's members in the listing
([[include=["users"]]]): all consumers of these groups go on to resolve
membership, and the embedded lists let them do so without one API call per
group (see [[group_member_ids]]).
The group category listing endpoint ignores this parameter, in which case
membership resolution falls back to [[get_users()]].
<<functions>>=
def process_group_options(canvas, args):
  """Processes the group/group category options, returns a list of groups"""

  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)

  if args.category:
    group_list = list(filter_groups(
      filter_group_categories(course_list, args.category),
      args.group,
      include=["users"]))
  else:
    group_list = list(filter_groups(
      course_list,
      args.group,
      include=["users"]))
  
  if not group_list:
    raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError("No groups found matching the criteria")
  return group_list
@

\subsection{An option for user or group}%
\label{sec:user-or-group-option}

Finally, we offer both selections through one function, so that a command
picks up [[-u]] and [[-G]] as alternatives with a single call.
This is where the distinction we drew at the start of the section earns its
keep.

The tempting implementation is to make a mutually exclusive group and hand
\emph{that} to the two [[_wo_depends]] helpers, rebinding [[parser]] on the
way:
\begin{quote}
[[parser = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=required)]]
\end{quote}
This is what we used to do, and it is wrong, because each helper adds
\emph{two} options: a selection \emph{and} its filter.
All four ended up in the group, and argparse then refuses any two of them
together:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[-u alice -r ta]] ] was rejected, although the role only says how to
look [[alice]] up.
\item[ [[-G Labb -C Labbgrupper]] ] was rejected, although
[[process_group_options]] implements precisely that case---narrow to the
category, then match group names inside it.
\end{description}
The requiredness went wrong too: since [[-r]] sat in the group, giving it alone
satisfied [[required=True]], so [[grade -r student]] passed the check while
selecting nobody.

The repair follows from the distinction: only the alternatives belong in the
group.
We keep both parsers alive under different names---the group for the
selections, the original [[parser]] for the filters---and let each helper route
its two options accordingly.
<<functions>>=
def add_user_or_group_option(parser, required=False, suppress_help=False):
  """Adds the user and group selection options to argparse parser

  The -u and -G selection options become mutually exclusive alternatives,
  while their -r and -C filter options go on parser itself, so that each
  filter can be combined with the selection it refines.

  Args:
    parser: The argparse parser to add options to
    required: Whether to require one of the user/group options (default: False)
    suppress_help: If True, hide these options from help output (default: False)
  """
  try:
    courses.add_course_option(parser, suppress_help=suppress_help)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  selection = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=required)

  try:
    add_user_option_wo_depends(selection, suppress_help=suppress_help,
      filter_parser=parser)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass

  try:
    add_group_option_wo_depends(selection, suppress_help=suppress_help,
      filter_parser=parser)
  except argparse.ArgumentError:
    pass
@

With only the alternatives in the group, [[required=True]] again means what it
says: the caller must name [[-u]] or [[-G]].
The price is that [[-C]] alone no longer satisfies it, because a filter selects
nothing on its own.
Commands that require a selection therefore want
[[-G '.*' -C Labbgrupper]]---which asks for \enquote{all groups in that
category} and now reads the way it behaves.

\subsubsection{Deciding which alternative was used}

The processing must return a list of users, so for the group alternative we
have to go on and extract them.
<<functions>>=
def process_user_or_group_option(canvas, args):
  """Returns a list of users, filtered either by user regex or by groups"""
  if args.group or args.category:
    <<refuse a user selection combined with group options>>
    <<return the users of the selected groups>>

  return process_user_option(canvas, args)
@

Note that either group option is enough to mean \enquote{the group
alternative}: [[-C]] without [[-G]] is the \enquote{whole category} case from
the previous subsection.

<<return the users of the selected groups>>=
users = list()
for group in process_group_options(canvas, args):
  group_users = list(group.get_users())
  for user in group_users:
    canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_user_attrs(user)
  users.extend(group_users)

if not users:
  raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError("No users found in the specified groups")
return users
@

One combination survives the restructuring unguarded.
Argparse can keep an option out of at most one mutually exclusive group, so
having [[-C]] combine with [[-G]] costs us its former exclusivity with
[[-u]]---the parser now accepts [[-u alice -C Labbgrupper]], which asks for one
user and for a set of groups at the same time.
We reject it here instead of silently honouring one side, because whichever
side we dropped would discard something the caller wrote: for [[grade]],
dropping [[-u]] would mean grading everyone in the category.

We recognise a genuine user selection by comparing against
[[DEFAULT_USER_REGEX]]; any other regex can only have come from [[-u]] on the
command line.
<<refuse a user selection combined with group options>>=
if args.user and args.user != DEFAULT_USER_REGEX:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "can't combine --user with --category, "
    "--category refines the group selection, not the user selection")
@

\subsubsection{Verifying which combinations the parser accepts}

The whole point of the restructuring is which command lines argparse lets
through, so that is what we test.
We build a real parser and check both the cases the old grouping got wrong and
the ones it got right, since a fix that loses the latter is no fix at all.

<<test functions>>=
class TestUserOrGroupOption:
    """Test how add_user_or_group_option combines selections and filters."""

    def parser(self, required=False):
        parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
        users_module.add_user_or_group_option(parser, required=required)
        return parser

    <<user or group option test methods>>
@

A filter must combine with the selection it refines.
These two used to fail, which made [[--role]] and [[--category]] unusable in
every command built on this helper.

<<user or group option test methods>>=
def test_role_filter_combines_with_user(self):
    args = self.parser().parse_args(["-u", "alice", "-r", "ta"])
    assert args.user == "alice"
    assert args.role == "ta"

def test_category_filter_combines_with_group(self):
    args = self.parser().parse_args(["-G", "Labb", "-C", "Labbgrupper"])
    assert args.group == "Labb"
    assert args.category == "Labbgrupper"
@

The selections, on the other hand, must stay alternatives; and when a
selection is required, only a selection may satisfy the requirement.

<<user or group option test methods>>=
def test_user_and_group_stay_exclusive(self):
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
        self.parser().parse_args(["-u", "alice", "-G", "Labb"])

def test_selection_satisfies_required(self):
    args = self.parser(required=True).parse_args(["-G", "Labb"])
    assert args.group == "Labb"

def test_filter_alone_does_not_satisfy_required(self):
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
        self.parser(required=True).parse_args(["-r", "student"])
@

Finally, the combination argparse can no longer rule out for us must still be
refused, now by [[process_user_or_group_option]] itself.

<<user or group option test methods>>=
def test_user_regex_with_category_is_refused(self):
    args = argparse.Namespace(user="alice", group=None,
                              category="Labbgrupper")
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
        users_module.process_user_or_group_option(None, args)
